  "doctrinal": "Teacher-only: Wisdom personified as a woman crying in the streets (1:20-33) is best read as vivid poetic personification of God's own wisdom and moral order, not as a distinct divine person standing alongside God. Some later Christian readers push straight from this figure to the pre-existent Christ of John 1's Logos — and there is a real canonical trajectory there (1 Corinthians 1:24,30 does call Christ 'the wisdom of God') — but Proverbs 1 itself doesn't present her as a separate hypostasis; she's a literary device for God's own summons, not a preview of the Trinity that the original audience would have recognized as such. I'd teach the personification for what it plainly is first, and only note the later New Testament resonance as a legitimate but secondary, retrospective connection.",
  "story": {
    "scene": "Picture a young man leaving his father's house for the first time, stepping out into the crowded gate-square of a town like Jerusalem or Beersheba on market day. The air is full of competing voices — merchants haggling over grain, elders settling a boundary dispute, and off to one side, a knot of confident, well-dressed young men laughing, waving him over with an easy promise: come with us, there's money in it, we'll share equally, no one will know. He hesitates. And then, cutting through the noise from the opposite direction, comes another voice — not private, not hidden in a corner, but shouting from the highest point of the gate itself, urgent and unashamed: 'How long, ye simple ones, will ye love simplicity?' It is a woman's voice, and she is not embarrassed to be heard by everyone. She is not selling anything. She is pleading, the way you plead with someone you can see is about to step off a ledge they haven't noticed.",
    "modern": "It's the difference between the group chat that says 'everyone's doing it, just don't tell anyone' and the quiet, unfashionable voice of a mentor, a parent, or your own conscience saying the same warning in public where it can be tested and examined. The pull of the first is that it feels like belonging and easy gain; the cost of the second is that it feels lonely and slow. Proverbs 1 insists the loud, communal, profitable-sounding invitation is usually the trap, and the unpopular voice standing in the open square, willing to be heard by everyone, is usually the one worth trusting — precisely because it isn't afraid of scrutiny."
  },
  "starterNotes": [],
