  "doctrinal": "Teacher-only: 5:22-23's picture of the wicked man being \"holden with the cords of his sins\" and dying \"without instruction\" reads, to me, as Proverbs describing how divine judgment ordinarily works itself out through the built-in moral structure of the world, rather than denying any future, direct divine verdict. I don't think this is pure cause-and-effect naturalism with God stepped back out of the picture — Proverbs elsewhere is emphatically clear that \"the ways of man are before the eyes of the LORD\" (5:21) — but I also don't think it should be spiritualized into meaning only a distant future judgment. The most honest reading holds both together: real consequences already begin unfolding in this life as part of how God has built moral cause and effect into the world, while a fuller reckoning still lies ahead. I'd resist a group's temptation to pick only one of those two truths and drop the other.",
