    "11:1-3 — Honest weights and the humility that walks with them. 'A false balance (moznei mirmah) is abomination to the LORD, but a just weight is his delight' (11:1) reaches back to the actual commerce of the marketplace: merchants used stone weights on a two-pan scale, and shaving or swapping the stones was the ancient equivalent of rigging a scale today. The proverb pairs this with an interior parallel: 'when pride cometh, then cometh shame, but with the lowly is wisdom' (11:2) and 'the integrity (tummah) of the upright shall guide them, but the perverseness of transgressors shall destroy them' (11:3). Cheating a scale and inflating your own self-estimate are treated as the same sin wearing two faces — both falsify a true measure, one of goods, one of the self.",
